S("S001","H001 gives λ≥0, so n=λ+1>0 and I consists of exactly n integers. We prove the string identities simultaneously by natural induction on t≥0. At t=0, hv=λv and xv=0 are H003. In the induction step temporarily assume L1: h y^t v=(λ−2t)y^t v and, when t≥1, x y^t v=t(λ−t+1)y^(t−1)v (with xv=0 at t=0). The relation hy=yh−2y from H002 gives h y^(t+1)v=(λ−2t−2)y^(t+1)v. The relation xy=yx+h gives x y^(t+1)v=y(x y^t v)+h y^t v=[t(λ−t+1)+λ−2t]y^t v=(t+1)(λ−t)y^t v, also at t=0 using xv=0. This proves the step and discharges L1; natural induction gives both identities for all t in their stated ranges.",
["H001","H002","H003"],[],["λ","n","I","h","y","x","v","t"],
[F("F001","n>0, |I|=n, and for all integers t≥0, h y^t v=(λ−2t)y^t v; for all integers t≥1, x y^t v=t(λ−t+1)y^(t−1)v.",["n","I","λ","h","x","y","v"],[{symbol:"t",type:"nonnegative integer"}])],["L1"],["L1"]),
S("S002","For each i∈I, repeated application of the x identity in F001 gives x^i y^i v=i!λ(λ−1)⋯(λ−i+1)v. At i=0 the product is empty and equals 1; at i≥1 its factors are positive integers, since i≤λ. Since v≠0 by H003, this shows e_i≠0 over ℂ. F001 gives h e_i=(λ−2i)e_i. To prove independence, fix coefficients c and temporarily assume L2: Σ_(i∈I)c_i e_i=0. Fix j∈I and apply ∏_(k∈I,k≠j)(h−(λ−2k)Id_V). Every term except j is zero; the j term is c_j [∏_(k≠j)2(k−j)]e_j. All its factors are nonzero complex numbers and e_j≠0, so c_j=0. Since j was arbitrary, all coefficients vanish. Discharging L2 proves independence.",
["H003"],["F001"],["I","e","x","y","h","v","λ","c","j","V"],
[F("F002","Every e_i, i∈I, is nonzero, h e_i=(λ−2i)e_i, and the family e:I→V is complex-linearly independent.",["I","e","h","λ","V"],[{symbol:"i",type:"element of I"}])],["L2"],["L2"]),
S("S003","Apply M001 to the division ring ℂ, the complex vector space V, the finite index set I and the family e. F002 supplies independence, F001 gives |I|=n, and H002 gives dim_ℂ V=λ+1=n and finite dimensionality. Thus the span of e is V, so together with independence it is a basis. By F001, y^(λ+1)v has eigenvalue −λ−2 under h. Expand this vector in the basis e; comparison of each coefficient in h y^(λ+1)v=(−λ−2)y^(λ+1)v gives [λ−2i−(−λ−2)] times that coefficient zero. For i∈I this multiplier is 2(λ+1−i)≠0 in ℂ. Every coefficient is zero, hence y^(λ+1)v=0. Applying further powers of y gives all higher vanishing.",
["M001","H002"],["F001","F002"],["V","e","I","n","λ","y","h","v"],
[F("F003","The family e indexed by I is a complex basis of V, y^(λ+1)v=0, and y^t v=0 for all integers t≥λ+1.",["e","I","V","y","λ","v"],[{symbol:"t",type:"integer at least λ+1"}])]),
S("S004","For arbitrary i∈I divide the identities F001 by i!: x e_0=0, and for i≥1, x e_i=(λ−i+1)e_(i−1). Directly y e_i=(i+1)e_(i+1) for i<λ, while y e_λ=0 by F003. Also h e_i=(λ−2i)e_i. These are the required integer action matrices.",
[],["F001","F003"],["i","I","e","x","y","h","λ"],
[F("F004","For i∈I: x e_0=0; x e_i=(λ−i+1)e_(i−1) if i≥1; y e_i=(i+1)e_(i+1) if i<λ; y e_λ=0; h e_i=(λ−2i)e_i.",["I","x","y","h","e","λ"],[{symbol:"i",type:"element of I"}])]),
S("S005","Fix r≥0. Iterating the x matrix in F004 and dividing by r! gives X(r)e_i=[(λ−i+1)⋯(λ−i+r)/r!]e_(i−r)=binom(λ−i+r,r)e_(i−r) when r≤i. When r>i, iteration reaches x e_0=0 and gives zero. Similarly iteration of y gives Y(r)e_i=[(i+1)⋯(i+r)/r!]e_(i+r)=binom(i+r,r)e_(i+r) if i+r≤λ, and gives zero past the terminating string. These binomial coefficients have nonnegative top at least r. They are integers: the factorial expressions have boundary values binom(a,0)=binom(a,a)=1 and satisfy binom(a,r)=binom(a−1,r−1)+binom(a−1,r) for 0<r<a by a common-denominator calculation; induction on the nonnegative integer a proves integrality. This establishes every divided-power formula in N001 as well as its zero cases.",
