argument:[
S("S001","Since p is prime by H001, p≥2 and the integers not divisible by p are multiplicatively closed. Fraction addition and multiplication therefore make R a subring of ℚ⊂ℂ; in particular every integer b with p∤b is a unit, with inverse 1/b∈R. By H004 choose a simultaneous integer basis B of M and complex basis B of V. It has finitely many vectors since V has finite dimension n by H002. M003 applied to its complex basis gives that its number of vectors is dim_ℂ V=n. By N001, |I|=n; reindexing the basis by I yields the existence stated in F001.",
["H001","H002","H004","M003","N001"],[],["p","R","M","V","n","B","I"],
[F("F001","There exists a family m:I→M which is a ℤ-basis of M and a complex basis of V.",["I","M","V"],[{symbol:"m",type:"family I→M"}])]),
S("S002","Choose m as in F001. Apply M002 with R₀=ℤ, A=M, S₀=R and the integer basis m. It gives the R-basis (1⊗m_i)_(i∈I) of M_R. The map ι_M sends these basis vectors to m_i; a finite R-linear relation among their images is also a complex-linear relation, so all coefficients are zero. Thus ι_M is injective, identifies M_R with the R-span of the m_i inside V, and its complex span is V. For any r≥0, admissibility H004 and D005 imply X(r)M⊂M and Y(r)M⊂M; R-linearity gives preservation of M_R. In particular X(1)=x and Y(1)=y preserve M; H002 gives h=xy−yx on V, so h preserves M as well. The commutators in H002 show directly that the integer span of x,y,h is bracket-closed, since all three structure constants are integers.",
["M002","H004","H002"],["F001"],["m","I","M","M_R","ι_M","V","R","X","Y","r","x","y","h"],
[F("F002","ι_M is injective; the vectors 1⊗m_i form an R-basis of M_R of cardinality n and their images span V over ℂ. For every integer r≥0, X(r),Y(r) preserve M and M_R. Also x,y,h preserve M, and ℤx+ℤy+ℤh is closed under brackets.",["ι_M","m","I","R","M_R","n","V","X","Y","M","x","y","h"],[{symbol:"r",type:"nonnegative integer"}])]),
S("S003","For every integer 0≤r≤λ, apply M006 with ℓ=p and u=r. H001 supplies primality and r≤λ<p, so p∤r!. Thus r! is a unit of R. In particular λ!, j!, (λ−j)! are all units, for j∈I. Their ratio binom(λ,j)=λ!/[j!(λ−j)!] is a unit of R, with inverse j!(λ−j)!/λ!. The factorials are positive and therefore nonzero as rational numbers. These statements also cover r=0.",
["M006","H001"],[],["r","j","I","λ","p","R"],
[F("F003","For all integers 0≤r≤λ, p∤r! and r! is a unit in R. For every j∈I, binom(λ,j) is a unit of R, with inverse j!(λ−j)!/λ!.",["p","λ","R","I"],[{symbol:"r",type:"integer with 0≤r≤λ"},{symbol:"j",type:"element of I"}])]),
S("S004","We now identify operators by their actions on the complex basis e from N001. In Y(λ)X(λ), every basis vector below e_λ is killed by X(λ); on e_λ, X(λ)e_λ=e_0 and Y(λ)e_0=e_λ. Hence Π=E_λλ. Next X(λ−i)e_λ=e_i, with coefficient binom(λ−λ+λ−i,λ−i)=1, giving X(λ−i)Π=E_iλ. For ΠY(λ−j), an input e_k with k<j is carried below e_λ and killed by Π; if k>j the lowering string terminates and gives zero; on e_j its coefficient is binom(λ,λ−j)=binom(λ,j) and its image is e_λ. Therefore ΠY(λ−j)=binom(λ,j)E_λj. All statements include λ=0, when every factor is the identity on the one-dimensional space.",
["N001"],[],["e","I","λ","Π","E","X","Y","i","j","V"],
[F("F004","For all i,j∈I, Π=E_λλ, X(λ−i)Π=E_iλ and ΠY(λ−j)=binom(λ,j)E_λj.",["I","Π","E","X","Y","λ"],[{symbol:"i",type:"element of I"},{symbol:"j",type:"element of I"}])]),
S("S005","Combining F004, or applying it to each e_k, gives X(λ−i)ΠY(λ−j)=binom(λ,j)E_ij. Invert the coefficient using F003 to get E_ij=binom(λ,j)^(-1)X(λ−i)ΠY(λ−j). Every factor on the right preserves M_R by F002 and R-linearity, hence every E_ij preserves M_R. With F002 and F003 this proves the entire statement N002.",
[],["F002","F003","F004"],["i","j","I","E","X","Y","Π","λ","R","M_R"])
],
